 ## Overview
-Empirical Translucent Volume Shading is an interactive optical model that approximates the qualitative appearance of translucency and volumetric shadows by adding a blurred indirect light contribution to the direct light term [S1]. Unlike physically accurate multiple scattering simulations, which are computationally prohibitive for interactive rates, this method focuses on visual fidelity—specifically color bleeding and diffusion of light across boundaries—rather than quantitative physical accuracy [S1]. It distinguishes itself from analytical approximations (such as the diffusion approximation) by imposing no restrictions on external illumination or optical properties, allowing it to work with arbitrary volumes and scanned data [S1].
+Empirical Translucent Volume Shading is an interactive optical model for volume rendering that captures the qualitative appearance of translucency and volumetric shadows by adding a blurred indirect light contribution to the direct light term [S1]. It solves the problem of rendering semi-transparent volumetric data with realistic lighting at interactive rates without requiring physically accurate multiple scattering simulations or homogeneous optical properties [S1]. Unlike physically accurate methods, it prioritizes visual fidelity over quantitative accuracy, and unlike analytical approximations, it imposes no restrictions on external illumination or complex optical properties [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method computes the final eye radiance by integrating a composite shading term along the view ray, weighted by transmittance and a total light intensity term that includes both direct attenuation and an empirical indirect component [S1].
+The method computes the final eye radiance by integrating a composite shading term along the view ray, where the shading term blends between direct attenuation and surface shading based on a scattering factor, and the light intensity includes both direct attenuation and a blurred indirect component [S1].
 
